1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"question-before-model","number":"02","title":"Explain the question before the model.","mode":"Hidden question","scale":"slide","dek":"Introduce a model by explaining the economic question it will answer. The audience can then understand why its assumptions and equations are needed.","before":[{"title":"Model entry · PDF p. 6","kind":"image","alt":"Production, capital accumulation, and a resource constraint introduce the model before its banking timeline.","image":"/originals/gertler-kiyotaki-p6.png","id":"production","sourcePage":"6"},{"id":"bank-balance","kind":"image","title":"Bank funding and balance sheet","image":"/originals/gertler-kiyotaki-p10.png","sourcePage":"10","alt":"Bank funding and balance sheet"},{"id":"bank-flows","kind":"image","title":"Interbank funding flows","image":"/originals/gertler-kiyotaki-p12.png","sourcePage":"12","alt":"Interbank funding flows"}],"after":[{"title":"Who finances firms when investment opportunities appear?","kind":"bankFunding","timeline":[{"label":"Before opportunities","text":"Households deposit funds with banks."},{"label":"After opportunities","text":"Banks redistribute funds through interbank lending."}],"nodes":["Bank without a new local opportunity","Bank with a new local opportunity","Local firms undertaking investment"],"edges":[{"from":0,"to":1,"label":"Interbank funding"},{"from":1,"to":2,"label":"Finance"}],"note":"Deposits are raised before banks learn where investment opportunities arrive.","label":"FUNDS AND INVESTMENT","revises":["bank-balance","bank-flows"]},{"title":"Local banks finance firms with three sources of funds.","kind":"equation","formula":"Q_t^h s_t^h=n_t^h+b_t^h+d_t","lines":["\\(Q_t^h s_t^h\\): value of claims on local firms","\\(n_t^h\\): net worth · \\(b_t^h\\): interbank borrowing","\\(d_t\\): household deposits, raised before opportunities arrive"],"note":"\\(h=i,n\\): investing or non-investing island; negative \\(b_t^h\\) means lending.","label":"THE BANK BALANCE SHEET","revises":["bank-balance","bank-flows"]}],"diagnosis":"The model starts with aggregate accounting; its funding problem becomes concrete several pages later. Bring that local purpose to the model entrance.","preserved":"The timing of deposits, local investment opportunities, interbank finance, and the bank balance sheet.","deferred":"Production and accumulation equations remain in the subsequent model section.","context":"PDF p. 2 already motivates financial intermediation and policy. This is a model-entry repair. The banking timing is on p. 10, flows on pp. 11–12.","sourceType":"public","source":{"authors":"Mark Gertler and Nobuhiro Kiyotaki","presenter":"Nobuhiro Kiyotaki","title":"Financial Intermediation and Credit Policy","date":"2010-06-22","url":"https://www.cirje.e.u-tokyo.ac.jp/research/workshops/macro/macropaper10/macro0622slides.pdf","pages":"6, 10, 12","event":"CIRJE/TCER Macroeconomics Workshop, University of Tokyo","eventURL":"https://www.cirje.e.u-tokyo.ac.jp/research/workshops/macro/macro2010.html"},"point":"Before introducing a model’s formal structure, explain the behavior or outcome you want to understand and the difficulty the model will help resolve. As you introduce agents, constraints, and choices, connect them to that purpose. This gives listeners a reason to follow the setup and a way to judge which details matter.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"","principle":"Give the audience a direction","topic":"Macro finance"}}]
